  "preamble": "Unrounding of the close back rounded vowel [u] gives the close central unrounded vowel [ɨ]. The classic case is early Slavic: Balto-Slavic long *ū lost its lip rounding and became Proto-Slavic *y, given as [ɨː] in historical surveys of Slavic. Prothesis of *v- before *ū- had already applied, so initial *ū- appears as *vy-. The vowel survives as ы in Russian and Belarusian and as y in Polish (Russian мышь [mɨʂ] and Polish mysz /mɨʂ/ 'mouse'), while Czech, Ukrainian and the South Slavic languages later merged it with i.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Unrounding, Centralization",
